    "What is your relationship with mathematics?": "I never studied math beyond upper secondary level, and I find it difficult to engage with advanced topics. I enjoy physics and economics because they connect to real-world issues, but math feels abstract and disconnected from my daily life. I’ve always seen it as a tool, not a subject I’m passionate about.",
    "Do you ever get anxious when thinking about mathematics?": "Yes, I do get anxious when I encounter math problems, especially when they involve complex equations or unfamiliar concepts. It’s not about intelligence—it’s about feeling overwhelmed. I try to take deep breaths and break problems into smaller parts, but anxiety still lingers when I don’t understand the logic behind a solution.",
    "Did you ever use AI to support your math learning in the last year? If yes, how was your experience?": "I used a simple AI app last year to check my basic algebra problems. It helped me verify answers and understand step-by-step solutions. However, I found it limited—no explanations for deeper concepts. It felt like a calculator more than a learning partner, and I didn’t feel confident relying on it for complex topics.",
    "How would you explain, step by step, how to solve a second order algebraic equation?": "First, write the equation in standard form: ax² + bx + c = 0. Then, use the quadratic formula: x = [-b ± √(b² - 4ac)] / (2a). Calculate the discriminant to see if solutions are real or complex. If real, plug in the values and simplify. This gives you two possible solutions, which you can verify by substituting back into the original equation.",
    "How would you explain, step by step, how to find the stationary points of an equation y=f(x)?": "To find stationary points, take the first derivative f'(x), set it equal to zero, and solve for x. These x-values are candidates for maxima or minima. Then, use the second derivative test: if f''(x) > 0, it’s a local minimum; if f''(x) < 0, it’s a local maximum. Plug the x-values back into f(x) to find the y-coordinates.",
